Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult the current development Record before acting.

The seventh physics block is installed after `@physics-entropy-review`. The exact new progression is `physics-rotation-motion`, `physics-angular-balance`, `physics-rotation-inertia`, shared `mech-mass-inertia`, `physics-rotation-work`, shared `mech-angular-momentum`, `physics-rotation-rolling`, `physics-rotation-exchange`, `physics-rotation-observation`, `physics-rotation-study`, `physics-rotation-review`. Nine new lessons and two reused mechanical lessons bring physics to **88 common learning Records**. Both shared Records retain their original imports, identities, prose and mechanical continuations; appended common reading notes scope their wider assignments. `mech-moving-frames` remains contextual preparation for later unrestricted three-dimensional dynamics. Earlier physical/mathematical investigations and programs are unchanged.

Accessible orientation begins with an observable mark, an unwrapped angle versus orientation modulo 2π, radians, angular velocity and angular acceleration. Differentiating r=R(cos θ,sin θ,0) derives v=Rωe_t and a=Rαe_t−Rω²e_r for a fixed axis. Existing oriented-circle, periodic-derivative and sampling lessons are linked at the point of use. An explicit component cross product introduces the three-dimensional construction without assuming a full vector-calculus course. Finite spatial orientations retain their noncommuting boundary.

Particle angular momentum L=r×p yields dL/dt=r×F in a fixed inertial frame. For a closed constant-mass system, internal torque cancellation requires central equal/opposite pair forces or a separately justified zero net internal torque; equal/opposite forces alone are insufficient. A shift c gives L_O′=L_O−c×P and τ_O′=τ_O−c×F. With absolute momenta about a moving point A, dL_A/dt=τ_A−v_A×P. At G, v_G=P/M makes the additional term vanish. The common balance explicitly distinguishes a moving contact point from a permanent fixed pivot.

The inertia development derives the thin-rod integral Mℓ²/12 and end-axis Mℓ²/3, reproducing the shared 0.16/0.64 kg·m² example. Equal midpoint masses give I_n=I_exact(1−1/n²), with an exact deficit and limit. Finite particle velocities v_i=V_G+ω×ρ_i give K=M|V_G|²/2+ωᵀI_Gω/2 and L_G=I_Gω because Σm_iρ_i=0. The tensor I=Σm_i[(ρ_i·ρ_i)Id−ρ_iρ_iᵀ] is symmetric and positive semidefinite: qᵀIq=Σm_i|q×ρ_i|². Ideal collinear mass can have zero inertia along its line; strict positivity is not claimed universally. Expanding the shifted tensor proves parallel-axis I_O=I_G+M[(c·c)Id−ccᵀ], including the scalar perpendicular-axis-separation interpretation. Mass and area moments retain different units and meaning.

Fixed-axis constant inertia gives τ_z=I_zzα, angular impulse ΔL_z=∫τ_zdt and work ΔK=∫τ_zdθ. An asymmetric fixed-axis body can require other support moments; the scalar reduction is not unrestricted τ=Iα. Changing inertia requires d(Iω)/dt, and body-fixed vector components require Iω_dot+ω×Iω. The constant-torque example I=2,τ=−3,ω₀=4,t=2 gives ω_f=1,Δθ=5,J=−6 and work −15 J, independently checked alongside the opening motion/origin examples.

The original rolling model declares positive forward translation and clockwise scalar rotation, with m,R,κ>0, I=κmR², center force F, applied couple M, signed contact force f and normal N>0. Equations ma=F+f, Iα=M−Rf and a=Rα give a=(F+M/R)/[m(1+κ)] and f=(M/R−κF)/(1+κ). Initial v=Rω and |f|≤μ_sN are required. The evaluator returns an admissibility flag; it does not label an inadmissible candidate as the actual slipping acceleration. For m=2,R=0.5,κ=0.5,N=20,μ_s=0.1, center force F=6 yields a=2 and f=−2 at the static limit. Couple M=3 with F=0 gives the same formal a but f=+4 and fails the limit. The total power identity is Fv+Mω; ideal contact power f(v−Rω) vanishes despite nonzero force. The incline extension gives μ_min=κ tan β/(1+κ); with κ=1/2,sin β=3/5,cos β=4/5,g=9.81, a=3.924 m/s² and μ_min=1/4. A sliding/contact law remains necessary after failure, and a stationary instantaneous contact is not assumed to be a fixed material pivot.

Two coaxial constant-inertia rotors exchange signed internal impulse J with no external axial impulse: ω₁′=ω₁−J/I₁ and ω₂′=ω₂+J/I₂. Their total L is unchanged, while Q=K_before−K_after=JD−SJ²/2 for D=ω₁−ω₂ and S=1/I₁+1/I₂. Synchronization requires J*=D/S and yields ω*=L/(I₁+I₂), Q*=I_red D²/2. Completing the square proves K=L²/[2(I₁+I₂)]+I_red D²/2 and the fixed-L minimum. The original I₁=2,I₂=3,ω₁=8,ω₂=−2 example has L=10,K_before=70; J*=12 gives common speed 2,K_after=10,Q=60. J=6 gives speeds 5 and 0,Q=45. J=24 gives speeds −4 and 6,Q=0, but is outside monotone passive synchronization. Endpoint energy nonincrease permits a larger impulse interval than simple frictional synchronization. A hypothetical viscous time law is separately derived; no time law, measured coupling or heat-transfer history is inferred from conservation alone.

Observation lessons distinguish conditional predictions from empirical evidence. Common final speed determines an inertia ratio under the stated assumptions when initial speeds differ, but common scaling of both inertias leaves that speed unchanged while scaling L and Q. A known independent external angular impulse and nonzero speed change could identify an axial inertia only with the other torque contributions accounted for. Angular sampling can hide turns; measured center acceleration alone cannot establish rolling contact admissibility. The independent extension asks for a nonuniform line-density integration, convergent mass approximation, coupled-rotor prediction, ambiguity analysis and critic-driven revision rather than hardware construction.

Primary-source inspection extended existing `fd-openstax` and `mech-tong-dynamics`, without new source identities or bundled media. Actual selected OpenStax passages in 10.4, 10.5, 11.1, 11.2 and the opening 11.3 statement were read. The bounded Example 10.8 reconstruction uses six 0.02 kg masses at ±0.25,±0.15,±0.05 m: I=0.0035 kg·m², removing the nearest pair gives 0.0034, and at five revolutions/s K=0.175π²≈1.73 J. The program sums individual tensor terms and retains the exact coefficient of π². The actual symbolic cylinder incline Example 11.1, rod integral and particle angular-momentum derivative were inspected. Moebs/Ling/Sanny and the 19 September 2016 publication identity remain distinct from current footer dates. Publisher CC BY-NC-SA and additional AI/commercial-printing notices are preserved. No exercise wording or illustrations were copied. Tong's actual author-hosted Michaelmas 2004/2005 PDF, selected printed pages 50–54 and body-frame passage were inspected; general adaptation rights remain unestablished. Full chapters, exercises and practical projects remain assigned learning, not claimed author accomplishments.

